WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.045
Here’s the whole idea behind Forge. This Python

00:00:03.045 --> 00:00:06.245
expression adds two, multiplies by three, and takes the

00:00:06.245 --> 00:00:09.801
square root. We’ll run the same nine numbers two ways

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:12.646
and compare what the CPU has to do.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:16.598
Start with fusion turned off. Forge finishes the add

00:00:16.598 --> 00:00:20.211
for every input and saves that array. Then it reads

00:00:20.211 --> 00:00:23.824
that array back for the multiply. The square root does

00:00:23.824 --> 00:00:27.438
the same thing, so there are three passes and two

00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:28.160
intermediate arrays.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.476
Turn fusion on and look at the new loop. Forge reads a

00:00:33.476 --> 00:00:36.937
group, does all three operations while those values are

00:00:36.937 --> 00:00:40.399
in CPU registers, and writes the final answers. The

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:43.476
output stays the same. Those two temporary arrays

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:43.860
disappear.
